  "full_text": "There's a story asking, can the human eye detect a single photon? And the question is, how does God control us, you know? Like, one possibility is, let's just say photons control our brains because God's that good, you know, or something like that. So, like... The other day I was like, I had my shirt, back in the summer I had my shirt off. And I was like, well, maybe I'm not going to get cancer, right? Because God's going to protect me. God could protect me from cancer. And then I got to thinking, is that true? Like, we all know that it's obvious God could cause cancer, right? He could aim a photon or a cosmic ray or whatever at a certain cell and cause it just the right destruction to make it mutate in just the right way to go malignant and spread. So God could cause someone to have cancer. But here's a really weird question. Can God cause someone not to have cancer? Because now, I don't know if God controls everything. Maybe he does. But it's a lot harder to cause no cancer than to cause cancer. At least it seems like it is. Because now you have to control all the photons hitting. And can God... I certainly know God can control one photon, but can he control all of them? Because if any of them hit and cause cancer, let's just say the immune system, he can make the immune system kill it. But isn't that weird? Like the thought that can God cause someone not to have cancer?",
